                      %mn 4
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{l}(S^4)
          %p
            will also have to be such that it somehow gives rise to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
          %p
            under (double) dimensional reduction. This is still an
            = succeed "." do
              %strong open problem
          %p For further comments see the talk
          %p
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Equivariant+cohomology+of+M2%2FM5-branes"} Equivariant cohomology of M2/M5-branes
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p Now that we have found
          %p
            the descended
            = succeed "-cocycles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
          %p
            for all super
            = succeed "-branes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p in twisted cohomology, rationally,
          %p
            we may analyze their behaviour under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
          %p
            and discover the super
            = succeed "-algebraic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
          %p
            of various
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualities+in+string+theory"} dualities in string theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h2#DoubleDimensionalReduction Double dimensional reduction
          %p
            Underlying most of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualities+in+string+theory"} dualities in string theory
            is the phenomenon of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"}> double dimensional reduction
            ” (
            %a{:href => "double+dimensional+reduction#DuffHoweInamiStelle87"}> Duff-Howe-Inami-Stelle 87
            ), so called because:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
                is reduced by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
                on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %li
              %p
                in parallel the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
                is reduced if they
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wrapped+brane"} wrap
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            We now explain a mathematical formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            of brane charges, following (
            %a{:href => "#FSS16b"}> FSS 16b, section 3
            ,
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#BMSS18"} BMSS 18, section 2.2
          %p First we give an exposition of the
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#DoubleDimensionalReductionIdea"} Idea
              \.
          %p Then we discuss a first approximation to a mathematical formalization:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#DoubleDimensionalReductionViaFreeLooping"} Via free looping (no 0-brane effect)
          %p and then the accurate and fully general formalization in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#DoubleDimensionalReductionViaCyclification"} Via cyclification (with 0-brane effects)
          %p
            We then specialize this general procedure to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-infinity+algebras"} super L-infinity algebras
            so that it applies to the super
            = succeed "-brane" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#DoubleDimensionalReductionLInfinity"} On super p-brane cocycles
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h3#DoubleDimensionalReductionIdea Idea
          %p
            The original example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            (
            %a{:href => "double+dimensional+reduction#DuffHoweInamiStelle87"}> Duff-Howe-Inami-Stelle 87
            ) is supposed to underly the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA string theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 11
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{11}
                is an 11d
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
                locally of the form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 11
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 10
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{11} =  X_{10} \times S^1
                over a 10d base spacetime;
            %li
              %p
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane"} membrane
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"}> M2-brane
                ) with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder"} cyclindrical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 3
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\Sigma_3 = \Sigma_2 \times S^1
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wrapped+brane"} wraps
                the circle fiber if its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mrow
                          %mi M
                          %mn 2
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ⟶
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 11
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\phi_{M2} \;\colon\; \Sigma_3 \longrightarrow X_{11}
              %p is of the form
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⟶
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 10
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\phi_{F1} \times \mathrm{id}_{S^1}
                      \\;\colon\;
                      \\Sigma_2 \times S^1 \longrightarrow X_{10} \times S^1
                      \\,.
          %p
            As the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian
            circumference of the circle fiber
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            tends towards zero this effectively looks like the 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            tracing out a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
            in 10-dimensional spacetime:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 10
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi_{F1} \;\colon\; \Sigma_2 \longrightarrow X_{10}
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            But there is also “single dimensional reduction” when the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane"} membrane
            does not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wrapped+brane"} wrap
            the fiber space:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Σ
                            %mn 3
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mrow
                                  %mi M
                                  %mn 2
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 11
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mrow
                                  %mi D
                                  %mn 2
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 10
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Sigma_3 &amp;&amp;  \overset{\phi_{M2}}{\longrightarrow} &amp;&amp; X_{11}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\phi_{D2}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; X_{10}
                  }
          %p
            In this case it looks like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane"} membrane
            in 10d
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D2-brane"} D2-brane
          %p
            Similarly the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mn 5
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 6
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 11
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi_{M5} \;\colon\; \Sigma_{6} \longrightarrow X_{11}
          %p
            may
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wrapped+brane"} wrap
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            to yield a 4-brane in 10d, called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D4-brane"} D4-brane
            or it may not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wrapped+brane"} wrap
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            to yield a 5-brane in 10d, called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NS5-brane"} NS5-brane
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            Beware the naive treatment of branes in this traditional argument. And even naively, this is not the full story yet: The
            = succeed "-fibration" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D0-brane"} D0-brane
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-brane"} D6-brane
          %p
            Hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA string theory
            is meant to, schematically, involve decompositions as follows
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi X
                                %mn 11
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mi π
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi X
                                %mn 10
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mi spacetime
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mtext M2-brane
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mtext M5-brane
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mrow
                                %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                  %mi wrapped
                              %mo ↙
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo ↘
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi not
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi wrapped
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mrow
                                %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                  %mi wrapped
                              %mo ↙
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo ↘
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi not
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi wrapped
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mtext D0-brane
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mtext F1-brane
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mtext D2-brane
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mtext D4-brane
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mtext NS5-brane
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mi branes
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{spacetime}{
                  \\underbrace{
                  \\array{
                  X_{11}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\pi}
                  \\\
                  X_{10}
                  }
                  }
                  }
                  \\;\;
                  \\underset{branes}{
                  \\underbrace{
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \text{M2-brane} &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \text{M5-brane}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{wrapped}}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{not \atop wrapped}} &amp;
                  \&amp;
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  {}^{\mathllap{wrapped}}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{not \atop wrapped}}
                  \\\
                  \\text{D0-brane} &amp;&amp; \text{F1-brane} &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp;  \text{D2-brane} &amp;&amp; \text{D4-brane} &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \text{NS5-brane}
                  }
                  }
                  }
          %p
            Above we saw that all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+p-branes"} super p-branes
            are characterized by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
            fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_{p+2}
            that they are charged under, more precisely by the bispinorial component of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_{p+2}
            which is constrained to be super-tangent-space-wise the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                    %mi fermionic
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi i
                          %mrow
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi p
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mover
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                        %mi ⋯
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi p
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mo )
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H_{p+2}^{fermionic}
                  \\;=\;
                  \\tfrac{i^{p(p-1)/2}}{p!} \, \left( \overline{\Psi} \wedge \Gamma_{a_1 \cdots a_p}\Psi \right) \wedge E^{a_1} \wedge \cdots \wedge E^{a_p}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E^a, \Psi^\alpha)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vielbein"} super vielbein
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitino"} gravitino
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            Hence we will formalize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            in terms of these
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fields"} fields
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            Again there is a naive picture to help the intuition: Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi cl
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 11
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_4 \in \Omega^4_{cl}(X_{11})
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential 4-form
            flux
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
          %p
            Under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gysin+sequence"} Gysin sequence
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spherical+fibration"} spherical fibration
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↪
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 11
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi π
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 10
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  S^1 &amp;\hookrightarrow&amp; X_{11}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\pi}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; X_{10}
                  }
          %p this decomposes in cohomology as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 4
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 10
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 3
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G_4 = (d x^{10}) \wedge H_3 + \pi^\ast F_4
          %p thus giving rise in 10d to
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a 3-form
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_3
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
                that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                couples to;
            %li
              %p
                a 4-form
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_4
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
                in degree 4, that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D2-brane"} D2-brane
                couples to.
          %p
            Similary the 7-form field strength
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 7
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_7
            decomposes as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 7
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 10
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 6
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 7
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G_7 = (d x^{10}) \wedge F_6 + \pi^\ast H_7
          %p thus giving rise in 10d to
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a 6-form
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 6
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_6
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
                in degree 6, that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D4-brane"} D4-brane
                couples to
            %li
              %p
                a 7-form
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mn 7
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_7
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NS5-brane"} NS5-brane
                couples to.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi X
                                %mn 11
